# Linux Commands Cheat Sheet
## Beginner to SOC Analyst

A practical reference for learning Linux commands, system administration, networking and cybersecurity fundamentals.

**Operating System:** Kali Linux / Ubuntu / Debian  
**Purpose:** Linux Fundamentals and SOC Analyst Preparation

---

# 1. Basic Linux Commands

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| `whoami` | Displays current username |
| `who` | Displays logged-in users |
| `pwd` | Shows current working directory |
| `date` | Displays current date and time |
| `cal` | Displays calendar |
| `clear` | Clears terminal |
| `history` | Displays previously executed commands |
| `man command` | Opens command manual |
| `command --help` | Displays command help |
| `exit` | Exits terminal session |

Examples:

```bash
whoami
pwd
date
history
man ls
ls --help
```

# 2. File and Directory Management

### Listing files

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| `ls` | Lists files and directories |
| `ls -l` | Detailed file listing |
| `ls -a` | Shows hidden files |
| `ls -la` | Detailed listing including hidden files |
| `ls -lh` | Displays file sizes in readable format |
| `ls -R` | Lists directories recursively |

### Navigating directories

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| `cd directory` | Enters a directory |
| `cd ..` | Moves one directory backward |
| `cd ~` | Moves to home directory |
| `cd /` | Moves to root directory |
| `cd -` | Returns to previous directory |

### Creating files and directories

```bash
mkdir projects
mkdir -p projects/linux/notes
touch notes.txt
```

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| `mkdir` | Creates a directory |
| `mkdir -p` | Creates nested directories |
| `touch` | Creates an empty file |
| `rmdir` | Removes an empty directory |

### Copying, moving and deleting

```bash
cp file.txt backup.txt
cp -r folder backup_folder

mv old.txt new.txt
mv file.txt folder/

rm file.txt
rm -i file.txt
rm -r folder/
```

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| `cp` | Copies files |
| `cp -r` | Copies directories recursively |
| `mv` | Moves or renames files |
| `rm` | Removes files |
| `rm -i` | Asks before deleting |
| `rm -r` | Removes directories recursively |

**Warning:** Avoid `rm -rf` unless you fully understand the target path. Deleted files may not be recoverable.

