# 11. Shell Operators, Pipes and Redirection

These are important for combining commands and analysing logs.

| Operator | Description |
|---|---|
| `>` | Redirects output and overwrites a file |
| `>>` | Appends output to a file |
| `<` | Takes input from a file |
| `|` | Passes output to another command |
| `&&` | Runs next command if previous succeeds |
| `||` | Runs next command if previous fails |
| `&` | Runs a process in background |

Examples:

```bash
ls > files.txt

echo "Linux Learning" >> notes.txt

cat < notes.txt

ps aux | grep ssh

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade

mkdir test || echo "Directory creation failed"

firefox &
```

# 12. SOC Analyst - Linux Log Investigation

Logs are records of system activities and events. SOC Analysts examine logs to identify suspicious activities and investigate security alerts.

### Important Linux log locations

| Location | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `/var/log/syslog` | General system logs on Debian-based systems |
| `/var/log/auth.log` | Authentication and authorization logs |
| `/var/log/kern.log` | Kernel-related logs |
| `/var/log/dpkg.log` | Package management logs |
| `/var/log/` | Main Linux log directory |

Note: Kali and other Linux distributions may use different logging configurations. Some systems use systemd journal instead of traditional log files.

### Viewing logs

```bash
ls -lah /var/log/

sudo cat /var/log/auth.log

sudo less /var/log/auth.log

sudo tail -f /var/log/auth.log

sudo journalctl

sudo journalctl -n 50

sudo journalctl -f

sudo journalctl -p warning
```

### Investigating failed login attempts

```bash
sudo grep -i "failed" /var/log/auth.log

sudo grep -i "invalid user" /var/log/auth.log

sudo grep -i "authentication failure" /var/log/auth.log
```

### Investigating successful logins

```bash
last

lastlog

who

w
```

### Searching for suspicious activity

```bash
sudo grep -i "error" /var/log/syslog

sudo grep -i "failed" /var/log/auth.log

sudo journalctl -p err

sudo journalctl --since "1 hour ago"
```

